Programs whose names share a String.hashCode, for the native link's tests (tests/native-link.scala). Scala
Native's code generator numbers a class's virtual methods and a trait's methods in the order of the set of the
signatures each is called with (Scala 2.13's mutable.HashSet), where two signatures of one hash stand in the order
the link added them in: these programs hold such signatures, called in more than one order.

src/          the programs: Collision.scala, a code review's program as it was reported (probe.Collision: an
              abstract class's two methods Aa and BB, of one hash), then one program per place a scope's called
              signatures take (collide.<shape>.Main): an abstract class's methods (virtual), a trait's
              (interface), a trait's methods with bodies, one class taking them and one overriding some
              (default), a class's fields (fields). Their names are made of the blocks "Aa" and "BB", whose
              hashes are equal (Aa, BB; AaAa, AaBB, BBAa, BBBB), called in an order drawn from a fixed seed,
              some calls in a second method so that the link meets them later. Then the overrides' shape
              (collide.overrides<i>.Main): two names of one hash, each called on an abstract class answered by
              two classes, in one of two overrides of an abstract `run` that one call reaches, so that only the
              order Scala Native's link iterates that call's targets in orders the two calls: the four pairs of
              a probe of the review's (Aa/BB, AaAa/BBBB, AaBB/BBAa, BBBB/AaAa), then eight drawn from the seed (a
              string and the same with one letter raised and the next lowered by 31). A script outside the
              tree wrote them; project.scala holds the build's directives
nir/          the .nir files of the programs, written by the compiler plugin of Scala Native 0.5.12 for Scala
              3.8.4 (scala-cli compile of src/, the files taken from the classes directory)
digests.txt   the SHA-256 and the lines of what Scala Native's own tools leave for each program, linked from nir/
              and the jars of tests/nir/digests.txt as tests/native-link/README says for its program: its link's
              dump (linked) and its lowered definitions (lowered), once as the build decides on threads and once
              linked with them
<main>.txt    of the first lowered dump, the definitions of the program's own classes, by the rule of
              tests/native-link.scala's definitionsOf: what a failure is read against

The expectations are Scala Native's bytes, made as tests/native-link/README says (two runs of its tools leave the
same bytes); no file here is written from teq's output.
